From 766ba9c1e34fedd32b2fac0f7037f42398eb52fe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Axel AHO <aho.axel5@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 23:58:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Make a seeded chaos run reproducible when deliveries overlap
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`Chaos` promises that "seed makes the fault stream deterministic so chaos
runs are reproducible in tests". `test_seeded_fault_streams_are_reproducible`
checks that two fresh `Chaos.rng()` objects yield the same sequence, which is
a property of `random.Random` rather than of the emulator; no test covered a
run.

It does not hold for a run. `inject` and `_fire` both end in
`asyncio.create_task(deliver(payload))`, so deliveries overlap, and
`deliver()` awaited `asyncio.sleep(delay)` between two draws from a single
`rng` shared by every delivery. Which coroutine drew next depended on the
event loop, so the same seed injected a different number of faults from one
run to the next as soon as more than one event was in flight.

Found running 20 doors at once against the `delivery` preset: seed 7 gave 8
drops on one run and 9 on the next.

Every draw for a delivery is now taken before the first await, so the stream
is consumed in whole per-delivery chunks. Which delivery is dropped still
depends on the order they start in and cannot not — `meta.request_id` is a
fresh uuid4, so a delivery has no identity that survives a restart — but the
counts a measurement rests on are reproducible again. The README says which
of the two the seed fixes.

Also, because both call sites kept no handle on the tasks they created, a
consumer had no way to know when the queue had drained. With the `delivery`
preset a delivery is held up to 1.8 s, so the only recourse was to sleep for
a guessed number of seconds; on a loaded machine that guess is short, and a
run reports fewer deliveries than were made. `GET /_sandbox/chaos` now
reports `pending`, and holding the reference also stops a task being
collected mid-flight.

README: count faults from `/_sandbox/chaos`, not from `/_sandbox/state`,
which truncates `delivered` to its last 50 entries and so undercounts
silently on any run of size.

Verified by mutation: reintroducing one await between the draws fails
`test_a_seeded_run_is_reproducible_under_concurrent_delivery`.

Nothing here was checked against the real Ring API — it is entirely about the
emulator's own behaviour.
